I think the idea is that if you know the grammar, you should be able to
understand what is being said, regardless of dialect. As long as what you
are saying is correct, does it matter in what style you say it?

And I am one of those who tends towards the terminator-centric part, but
don't shy away from {cu} like some others do. I just feel it's another tool
in the box. My qualms with {cu} come far more from a teaching perspective,
where I feel that it's relied upon too heavily, and it becomes a crutch that
leads to sloppy terminator use - but I'm digressing here.

I guess what I should say is that learning lojban is quite often a rather
substantial paradigm shift in the way you think about language. I would
actually consider your dual-language upbringing as a bonus! Run with it, and
use it to adapt yourself to better learning lojban.

We are always here to help.

mi'e .kribacr. mu'o

